WEBVTT

NOTE
English captions for WLS's broadcast of Herbert Morrison and Charles Nehlsen's
recording of the Hindenburg disaster (Northwestern University Libraries). Transcribed
by speech recognition and corrected by hand: names from the record and the broadcast.
[unclear] marks words that could not be told.

1
00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.360
Ladies and gentlemen, in place of a
program originally scheduled for this

2
00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.520
period, we present now one of the most
unique broadcasts we have ever presented,

3
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:21.920
an actual eyewitness description of the
disaster at Lakehurst, New Jersey last

4
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:24.060
night, the destruction of
the giant airship Hindenburg.

5
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.460
In keeping with the policy of the National
Broadcasting Company and affiliated

6
00:00:28.460 --> 00:00:34.560
stations to report a noteworthy event,
station WLS, the Prairie Farmer station in

7
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.340
Chicago, sent announcer Herbert Morrison
and engineer Charles Nehlsen to Lakehurst

8
00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:43.160
yesterday to record an
eyewitness description of

9
00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.660
the landing of the Hindenburg.

10
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:48.840
The occasion was the first anniversary
of the inauguration of transatlantic air

11
00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:53.460
service and the first
portions of the 1937 season.

12
00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:57.180
Morrison and Nehlsen were at Lakehurst
with their recording apparatus when the

13
00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:01.600
giant airship floated in, and they were
hard at work describing the scene and

14
00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:05.940
making a reference recording of
it when the disaster occurred.

15
00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:10.020
Both of these men are in the studio with
us now with a copy of the reference record

16
00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.980
which describes in detail their
observations and thoughts as they

17
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.800
witnessed and recorded this catastrophe.

18
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:19.460
In order that you may understand the
picture a little more thoroughly before we

19
00:01:19.460 --> 00:01:23.480
play the record, I'm going to ask Herb
Morrison, whose voice will be heard on

20
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.260
this record, to tell us something of the
scene that first greeted them on their

21
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:31.420
arrival at Lakehurst. Uh, Herb, would you
tell us, was there anything unusual about

22
00:01:31.420 --> 00:01:34.040
the scene? By that I mean, did
you, did you have any [unclear]

23
00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.160
of danger? No, Bob, it had been raining
30 minutes before the arrival of the

24
00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.220
Hindenburg, and the only difficulty that
anyone thought was, uh, maybe a little

25
00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.560
extra work would be needed
to land the ship in the rain.

26
00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:47.920
Now, [unclear], tell me, had the rain
stopped before the Hindenburg came in for

27
00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:51.600
the landing? No, Bob, it wasn't
raining hard, just a slight drizzle.

28
00:01:51.600 --> 00:01:55.840
We had our recording apparatus set up
outside the hangar, the heavier-than-air

29
00:01:55.840 --> 00:01:59.340
hangar, and I turned to Charlie Nehlsen
and said, “Charlie, I'm going outdoors for

30
00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.220
the description of the landing,” and at
this point my voice on the record will

31
00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.720
tell you what happened. The ship is riding
majestically toward us, like some great

32
00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.940
feather, riding as though it was mighty
good, mighty proud of the place it's

33
00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:12.680
playing in the world's aviation.

34
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.440
The ship is no doubt bustling
with activity, as we can tell.

35
00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:18.380
Orders are shouted to the crew, the
passengers are probably lining the windows

36
00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:21.340
looking down ahead of them, getting
their glimpse of the mooring mast.

37
00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:25.160
And these giant flagships standing here,
the American Airlines flagships, waiting

38
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:27.800
to rush them to all points in the United
States when they get the ship moored.

39
00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:32.400
There are a number of important persons
on board, and no doubt the new commander,

40
00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:36.600
Captain Max Pruss, is thrilled too, for
this is his great moment, the first time

41
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:39.960
he's commanded the Hindenburg, for
on previous flights he acted as chief

42
00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:41.460
officer under Captain Lehmann.

43
00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:43.660
It's practically standing still now.

44
00:02:43.660 --> 00:02:45.880
They've dropped ropes out
of the nose of the ship,

45
00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:50.260
and they've been taken a hold of
down on the field by a number of men.

46
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.660
It's starting to rain again.

47
00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:55.100
The rain had slacked up a little bit.

48
00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.640
The back motors of the ship are
just holding it, just enough to keep

49
00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:02.140
it from… It burst into flames!

50
00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:05.360
Get this, Charlie! Get this, Charlie!

51
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.160
It's crashing! It's crashing!

52
00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.120
It's terrible! Oh, my! Get
out of the way, please!

53
00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:14.580
It's burning, bursting into flames, and
it's falling on the mooring mast, and all

54
00:03:14.580 --> 00:03:16.750
the folks between us. This is terrible!

55
00:03:16.750 --> 00:03:19.580
This is one of the worst
catastrophes in the world!

56
00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.930
Oh, it's, it's, it's [unclear], oh, four
or five hundred feet into the sky, and

57
00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.370
it's a terrific crash,
ladies and gentlemen.

58
00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:30.940
It's smoke, and it's flames now, and the
frame is crashing to the ground, not quite

59
00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.860
to the mooring mast. Oh, the humanity,
and all the passengers [unclear]

60
00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.760
around here! I told you, [unclear].

61
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:46.640
I can't talk, ladies and gentlemen.

62
00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.820
Honest, it's just laying there, a mass of
smoking wreckage, and everybody can hardly

63
00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:54.440
breathe and talk, and
[unclear]. I'm sorry.

64
00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.800
Honestly, I can hardly breathe.

65
00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:02.600
I'm going to step inside, where I
cannot see it. Charlie, that's terrible.

66
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:10.700
I, I, listen, folks, I'm going to have to
stop for a minute, because I've lost my

67
00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.700
voice. This is the worst
thing I've ever witnessed.

68
00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.740
… And I'm back again. I've, I've sort of
recovered from the terrific explosion and

69
00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:22.880
the terrific crash that occurred just as
it was being pulled down to the mooring

70
00:04:22.880 --> 00:04:27.320
mast. They're still smoking and flaming
and crackling and banging down there, and

71
00:04:27.320 --> 00:04:29.640
I don't know how many of the ground
crew were under when it fell.

72
00:04:29.640 --> 00:04:32.860
There's not a possible chance
for anyone to be saved.

73
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:36.940
The relatives of the people who are
waiting here, ready to welcome their loved

74
00:04:36.940 --> 00:04:38.800
ones off this great ship,

75
00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.960
are broken up. They're carrying them in
to give them first aid, and [unclear], and

76
00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:46.360
some of them have fainted.

77
00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.540
And the people are rushing
down to the burning ship.

78
00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:55.660
The fire trucks have all gone down to see
if they can extinguish any of the blaze

79
00:04:55.660 --> 00:05:02.120
whatsoever, but the terrible amount of
hydrogen gas in it, it just caused the

80
00:05:02.120 --> 00:05:06.600
tail surface to burst into flame first,
then there was a terrific explosion, and

81
00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:10.500
that followed by the burning of the nose
and the crashing of the nose into the

82
00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:13.040
ground, and everybody's
running back [unclear]

83
00:05:13.040 --> 00:05:16.720
to get out from underneath it, because it
was over the people at the time it burst

84
00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:21.180
into flame. Now, whether it fell on the
people witnessing it, we do not know, but

85
00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.320
as it exploded, they ran back, and now
it's smoking, a terrific black smoke

86
00:05:26.320 --> 00:05:27.260
floating up into the sky.

87
00:05:27.260 --> 00:05:33.580
The flames are still leaping maybe 30, 40
feet from the ground, the entire 811-foot

88
00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:38.980
length of it. They're frantically
calling for ambulances and things.

89
00:05:38.980 --> 00:05:47.220
The wires are humming with activity, and,
uh, I've, I've lost my breath several

90
00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:49.560
times during this exciting moment here.

91
00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.740
And will you pardon me just a moment?

92
00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.220
I'm not going to stop talking.

93
00:05:53.220 --> 00:05:56.020
I'm just going to swallow several
times until I can keep on.

94
00:05:57.840 --> 00:06:04.940
I should imagine that the nose is not more
than 500 feet or maybe 700 feet from the

95
00:06:04.940 --> 00:06:12.180
mooring mast. They had dropped two ropes,
and whether or not some spark or something

96
00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.160
set it on fire, we don't know, or whether
something pulled loose on the inside of

97
00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:20.580
the ship, causing a spark and causing
it to explode in the tail surface.

98
00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:24.980
But everything crashed to the ground, and
there's not a possible chance of anybody

99
00:06:24.980 --> 00:06:31.540
being saved. I wish I could stop just
a moment and see if I can get my breath

100
00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:34.780
again. And Charlie, if you'll fade it out
just a minute, I'll come back with more.

101
00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:38.000
Ladies and gentlemen, I'm back again.

102
00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.320
I raced down to the burning ship, and just
as I walked up to the ship, [unclear], I

103
00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.000
met a man coming out. He
couldn't find his way.

104
00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:50.620
I grabbed a hold of him.

105
00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:55.120
It's Mr. Mangone, Philip
Mangone of New York.

106
00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.320
Mr. Mangone. He's burned terribly in the
hands, and he's burned terribly in the

107
00:06:59.320 --> 00:07:05.780
face, his eyebrows and all the hair burned
off, but he's walking and talking, and he

108
00:07:05.780 --> 00:07:09.040
told me he jumped. He jumped
with other passengers.

109
00:07:09.040 --> 00:07:11.340
Now, Mr. Spah, it sounds like, they say.

110
00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:18.980
We're not sure of it. He also got out, and
we notice the lines, the different lines,

111
00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:22.480
the airship line and the American Airways.

112
00:07:22.480 --> 00:07:26.080
The ambulances are down there, and
they're taking people out of the wreckage.

113
00:07:26.080 --> 00:07:30.560
It seems that a number of them jumped
clear when the explosion occurred in the

114
00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:35.580
tail. Now, I've just been running up with
this man, Mangone, and put him in a car.

115
00:07:35.580 --> 00:07:39.780
His wife and daughter met him, and I put
him in the car with him and sent him to

116
00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:42.780
the field hospital with the other
passengers who have been saved.

117
00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:47.720
Now, the mass of wreckage is still
burning, and I want to repeat again

118
00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.520
something that I may not have got clear,
have gotten clear, in the hysteria of the

119
00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:57.420
moment. The explosion occurred in the tail
surfaces, in the fins, the part that was

120
00:07:57.420 --> 00:08:02.940
highest after it had nosed
down to the mooring mast.

121
00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:07.120
Now, whether something slipped in the back
and caused a spark to set off the gas, we

122
00:08:07.120 --> 00:08:10.440
do not know. You see, they're using
hydrogen in this, or they were using

123
00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.080
hydrogen in this ship, and
it is extremely explosive.

124
00:08:14.080 --> 00:08:16.240
In fact, they wear [unclear]

125
00:08:16.240 --> 00:08:21.440
shoes when walking among the rafters
around the, uh, hydrogen gas bags.

126
00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:25.800
Now, something may have slipped, causing
a spark to set off some hydrogen which had

127
00:08:25.800 --> 00:08:28.020
leaked out into the structure.

128
00:08:28.020 --> 00:08:31.880
It's still burning. I don't know what
is burning, evidently part of the cargo.

129
00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.140
It's still flaming, 10 or 15 feet high.

130
00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:37.640
They're still rushing people out of it.

131
00:08:37.660 --> 00:08:44.380
Now, it is my sincere hope that as many as
possible, and as many as I think are out,

132
00:08:44.640 --> 00:08:48.640
I think the majority of the passengers
jumped when it came close to the ground,

133
00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.480
according to what Mr. Mangone told me.

134
00:08:50.740 --> 00:08:54.380
He says, “Thank God I jumped,”
and we say thank God for him also.

135
00:08:55.340 --> 00:08:58.420
And they're still smoking,
the wreckage is smoking.

136
00:08:58.420 --> 00:09:03.160
They've wired all available sources for
fire apparatus and everything to come

137
00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:08.920
rushing in, and doctors and nurses to come
down to take care of the people that are

138
00:09:08.920 --> 00:09:11.680
being taken out of the burning wreckage.

139
00:09:11.680 --> 00:09:17.480
Now it lays, 811 feet of
just smoldering ruins now.

140
00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:18.720
I couldn't talk when I saw it.

141
00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:24.920
I hope that it isn't as bad as I made
it sound there at the very beginning.

142
00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.680
Now, while I get my breath, I'm going to
check and see if there are any more saved

143
00:09:28.680 --> 00:09:31.270
out of it. All right, Charlie,
will you please fade it

144
00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:32.770
out, and I'll come in [unclear].

145
00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:40.040
All right, ladies and
gentlemen, well, we're back

146
00:09:40.040 --> 00:09:41.540
with another important bulletin.

147
00:09:41.840 --> 00:09:44.760
Mr. O'Laughlin, from the
Consumers Company of Chicago, was

148
00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:46.260
one of the passengers aboard

149
00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.480
the, uh, airship when it burst into
flames, and he managed to get free of the

150
00:09:50.480 --> 00:09:52.280
wreckage, and he's standing
here right by my side.

151
00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:57.840
I can't ask him to [unclear], but he wants
me to tell the folks that he is all right.

152
00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:00.160
Mr. O'Laughlin of the
Consumers Company, Chicago.

153
00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.900
And here is another man coming
in. He's burned considerably.

154
00:10:02.900 --> 00:10:05.600
Mr. Otto [unclear]. Mr. Otto [unclear]

155
00:10:05.600 --> 00:10:08.640
is safe, although he's
burned quite badly. Now,

156
00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:11.960
[unclear]. Thank you. Thank you.

157
00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.940
He's sitting right here.

158
00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:16.380
And is that his [unclear] with him?

159
00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.820
[unclear]. Mr. Otto, how
did you manage to get out?

160
00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:54.000
[He answers, his words unclear]

161
00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:57.520
Yes. When the flash came, he jumped out.

162
00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:01.280
He jumped out, and he
didn't get hurt a bit.

163
00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:03.960
No, he isn't hurt a bit, not hurt at all.

164
00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.520
Well, I'm so thankful for you.

165
00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:06.760
I'm so thankful for you.

166
00:11:06.760 --> 00:11:10.780
And, uh, can you tell him in your language
that we're thankful that he got out?

167
00:11:11.940 --> 00:11:15.620
And, uh, now, Charlie, fade it
out just a little bit, till I

168
00:11:15.620 --> 00:11:17.120
can get some more information.

169
00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:24.540
And now, friends, I want
to tell you, I'm back here.

170
00:11:24.540 --> 00:11:27.180
I want to tell you that the wreckage
is still flaming out there, but I have

171
00:11:27.180 --> 00:11:28.680
some very good news for you.

172
00:11:29.140 --> 00:11:33.080
I just came from the front of the building
where they have set up an emergency

173
00:11:33.080 --> 00:11:38.540
station, and they claim that between 25
and 30, that is the estimate, between 25

174
00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:41.200
and 30, are saved out of the wreckage.

175
00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:45.340
Now, I have here a total of
39 passengers and 61 crew.

176
00:11:45.340 --> 00:11:51.440
That would make an even 100, but they tell
me there were about 106 on board, so that

177
00:11:51.440 --> 00:11:58.960
makes between 25 and 30 out of 106
so far saved and accounted for.

178
00:11:58.960 --> 00:12:01.320
That is, who have been identified.

179
00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:05.000
It's still smoking. They can't get
to the back part of the wreckage yet.

180
00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:12.000
It would be impossible, because the flames
are, let's see, about 10 or 15 feet, still

181
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:17.120
leaping in the air. The front part of the
ship is intact for about 30 feet back.

182
00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:18.620
The nose of it is intact.

183
00:12:19.620 --> 00:12:24.480
It evidently had exploded and burned
before that part of the ship struck the

184
00:12:24.480 --> 00:12:28.620
ground. Even though it was the first part
of the ship that should have struck the

185
00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:30.920
ground, it didn't. The
burning mass [unclear]

186
00:12:30.920 --> 00:12:35.660
the back of the wreckage into the air,
and that, I think, is what saved the

187
00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:39.680
passengers for the simple reason
that it gave them a time to jump.

188
00:12:39.680 --> 00:12:43.780
If the explosion had occurred in the front
of the airship, they wouldn't have had

189
00:12:43.780 --> 00:12:46.900
time to jump, because it would
have blown the cabin right out

190
00:12:46.900 --> 00:12:48.400
of the bottom of the airship.

191
00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:53.700
But inasmuch as the flaming mast
held the wreckage in the air,

192
00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:58.740
that made it possible for them to jump,
and we are so thankful, because from where

193
00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:03.260
we could see, we thought at first every
one of them and the ground crew were gone.

194
00:13:03.320 --> 00:13:07.880
But I guess the ground crew had a chance
to get out, and it was back, just a little

195
00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:09.660
bit back of the [unclear]

196
00:13:09.660 --> 00:13:13.940
line, so that none of the spectators
got underneath the wreckage.

197
00:13:14.420 --> 00:13:18.240
We're so thankful for that, and in
checking, not a single spectator, only the

198
00:13:18.240 --> 00:13:21.000
persons engaged in the
lowering of the giant ship and

199
00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:22.500
the people who were on board.

200
00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:27.780
Now, we have not received any information
as to whether the crew escaped, any of the

201
00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.600
crew escaped, because they were evidently
up in the working part of the ship and

202
00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.200
wouldn't be down in the [unclear] part.

203
00:13:34.900 --> 00:13:38.680
Ladies and gentlemen, you've been
listening to an actual eyewitness

204
00:13:38.680 --> 00:13:42.540
description of the disaster to the airship
Hindenburg at Lakehurst last night.

205
00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:47.640
This recording you heard was made by
Herbert Morrison and Charles Nehlsen of

206
00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:50.280
station WLS, the Prairie
Farmer station, Chicago,

207
00:13:50.800 --> 00:13:53.880
who happened to be in person at the
airport at Lakehurst, together with their

208
00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:57.300
recording equipment, when the
disaster, the catastrophe, occurred.

209
00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:01.260
Herbert Morrison, in person, has
been here in the studio with us.

210
00:14:01.260 --> 00:14:05.360
This special presentation has come to
you through the Blue Network of the

211
00:14:05.360 --> 00:14:06.860
